## Build order and rollout [PLAN-NS]

- part-of: [FL-NORMATIVE-SOURCE]
- status: draft
- x-order: One change adds the seven keys, the confirmation keys and the RESTATES, CONFIRMS and CHECKED_AGAINST rows to the parser's tables, mints normative_effective, and brings the SPEC.md text and its absence test with it. Lint ([MECH-NS-LINT]) follows. The counting consumers ([MECH-NS-COUNTING]) follow lint, each when its owner has said yes.
- x-before-use: No strict document may use a new key before the change that makes it core lands, or it gets undeclared_key errors. This file lands with that change, so it is the first document to use them.
- x-amendment-step: The route is decided: the stakeholder selected building and landing this spec as a §16 amendment ([DD-NS-AMENDMENT-ROUTE]), conditional on a clean spec audit and a cross-systems review ([DD-NS-BUILD-SELECTED]). Both conditions must be in hand before the land; this file does not record either as done. The maintainers still rule on [OPEN-NS-CORE-COUNT].
- x-sdlc: In the same commit as the build, SDLC 7.6 gains a rule for each extension point of this spec. They are drafted with the engine owner at build time. The nine (the eight below, and a ninth added with the build for the confirmation locator; later rounds added more, all in SDLC 7.6): (1) a tool or lint that counts requirements, acceptance criteria or test targets filters on normative_effective, or is listed as open; (2) a new core key is added in one place and every key list (parser tables, writer accept-list, SPEC §1.3, profile collision guard) is derived from it or pinned by a test; (3) an edge type minted by a key (RESTATES, CONFIRMS and CHECKED_AGAINST among them) has one registration, its row in REL_PROPERTY_EDGES, and the reserved set the profile guard reads is derived from it as REL_PROPERTY_EDGES minus CORE_EDGE_LAYER, so no second hand-kept table exists; (4) a writer that moves or splits a section keeps the parent-section chain, and a test shows normative_effective unchanged; (5) a lexical lint states its limits and is pinned by a planted positive and a planted negative control; (6) source statement text and confirmation nodes are exempt from, or counted by, the semantic card builder as a stated choice, and any change to the cards bumps CARD_SCHEMA_VERSION; (7) the typed writers refuse to write a reserved minted property such as normative_effective, and a test shows the refusal writes nothing; (8) audits read non-normative content: leaving it out is for counting and building only, and a spec audit following the documented audit steps reads every source statement and confirmation.
- x-rollback: Engine rollback only; no data format changes. Under the old engine a document that uses the keys parses them as ordinary properties, a strict one reports undeclared_key, and restates mints no edge.
- x-migration: See [OPEN-NS-MIGRATION].

## Open: whether a write into a failed corpus is flagged in its receipt [OPEN-NS-FAILED-CORPUS-WRITE-FLAG]

- part-of: [FL-NORMATIVE-SOURCE]
- status: draft
- x-label: O4
- x-open: Whether a write by path into a corpus that is registered but failed to load (for any reason: an unreadable member, an unresponsive root, a refused profile) carries a flag in its receipt saying the corpus is not served. Today it lands and the receipt reads as for a healthy corpus, on the trunk and with this spec, for every failure kind. Later work, for all failure kinds; this spec asks nothing of it.
- x-owner: The engine maintainers.

## Decision: yes and no are aliases of true and false for normative [DD-NS-YES-NO-ALIAS]

- part-of: [FL-NORMATIVE-SOURCE]
- status: draft
- x-label: D6
- x-state: decided
- x-decision: The values yes and no of the key normative are aliases of true and false, normalised where normative_effective is minted (one place in the parser), accepted in any case (as are true and false themselves). A node or section marked no leaves normative scope exactly like false, and yes behaves exactly like true, including the override error inside a non-normative section. There is no value finding and no per-node spelling finding for the alias; lint says it at most once per file, as one info-level line suggesting the canonical spelling. Any other free text keeps its finding. The cross-product question was: the specs authored to date use yes and no (measured over the registered corpora: 83 of the 84 uses are in the public specs of one product, and the 84th, a free-text one, is in a different corpus and product; the private corpora hold none; the architects report the same spelling in drafts that are not registered, which was not measured here), and a forced migration costs a constrained resource for no gain in meaning. This is the authors' design decision, taken on the chief architect's cross-product ruling; it is not the stakeholder's selection.
- rationale: All but one use of the key spell it yes or no, as far as it was measured: over every registered corpus (the 39 public corpora and the 16 private ones queried by name, 2026-11-16 at 23:56Z) there are 84 uses of normative: 83 of the 84 are in the public specs of one product (66 no, 17 yes), and the 84th, the free-text one, is in a different corpus and product; the private corpora hold none. The architects report the same spelling in drafts that are not registered; that was not measured here. A forced migration would cost a constrained resource for no gain in meaning, and rejecting the old spelling would be strictness with no reader. A no that was read as free text left its quotes section in normative scope, so the quote tripwire fired on the quotes themselves (measured on one such spec: 32 bad-value warnings and 33 tripwire warnings, 9 of them genuine).
